{
  "_source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Signs_of_AI_writing (retrieved 2026-07-21)",
  "_note": "Signal lists for the NB5xx 'signs of AI writing' rules. Curated to favor precision; these are tells, not proof.",
  "_moved_to_authority": "'studies show', 'research suggests', 'experts argue', 'observers note' and 'industry leaders agree' left the editorializing list for data/authority.json (NB316). They are not primarily machine tells — people have written them forever — so they belong to a default-on rule rather than one that needs --ai. Do not add them back here: NB316 generalizes them by lemma, so a duplicate would fire twice on the same span. 'a leading expert in' and 'industry reports' stayed, because those read as puffery describing a named person or thing rather than an appeal to an unnamed source.",
  "_updated": "2026-07-23 — tells migrate with each model generation; a frozen list scans for last season's patterns. Refresh against the detection literature (and re-run the corpus calibration) every release or two.",
  "_credits": "Several entries adapted from conorbronsdon/avoid-ai-writing (MIT), which in turn credits blader/humanizer, Aboudjem/humanizer-skill, brandonwise/humanizer, and tropes.fyi. Vocab additions 2026-07-23 cross-checked against lynote-ai/humanize-text (MIT) postprocess word list.",
  "_exceptions": "The *_exceptions lists suppress matches inside fixed expressions ('quite a few', 'test harness'). When adding a new term to a list, check its idioms and literal senses — add them to the matching *_exceptions list and cover both directions in tests/test_ai_writing.py (see test_puffery_still_flags_verb_sense).",
